  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Sat Jun 6 2026\n\n- Isolated Thunderstorms Possible Today\n\nThe line of thunderstorms currently moving through the area will\nexit by 5am. Instability builds back in during the day with the\nmean HREF surfaced based CAPE values around 1000 J/kg and shear\naround 35 knots. Even with these values the HREF members are not\nexcited about thunderstorm chances later this morning and\nafternoon. Other guidance has also decreased on convection later\ntoday. This could be due to the lack of lift in the lower levels,\nbut with 500mb positive vorticity advection and the front moving \nthrough, will keep a 20 to 30 percent chance with the best \npotential east of I-69.\n\n\n- Increasing Chances for Rain and Thunderstorms Monday into\n  Tuesday\n\nThe upper level ridge builds back in for Sunday, but the shortwave\nover Texas will weaken the ridge bringing with it rain and\nthunderstorms for Monday and Tuesday. Heavy rain will be possible\nwith precipitable water values around 2 inches. Currently we are\nin the day 5 Excessive Rainfall Outlook from WPC. Depending on the\nevolution of the shortwave, rain and thunderstorms could linger\ninto Wednesday.\n\n- Warm and Humid With Slight Chances (20 percent) for\n  Thunderstorms\n\nTemperatures gradually warm through next week with the best\npotential for 90s on Thursday as 925 mb temperatures will be in\nthe 24 to 25 C range. However within the southwest flow and the\nhumid environment with dew points in the 60s and 70s,\nthunderstorms chances could play a role in how warm we actually\nget. Still worth monitoring as heat indices could be in the 90s \nmultiple days in a row and may approach the low 100s.",
